Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8; format=flowed
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Dear developers of the Kafka java client,

It seems I have found a feature gap in the Kafka java client.=20
KAFKA-10337 and its associated pull request on Github (from 2020!) would=20
solve this, but it was closed without merging. We would love to see it=20
being reconsidered for merging. This mail has the arguments for doing so.

The javadoc of `ConsumerRebalanceListener` method `onPartitionsRevoked`=20
recommends you commit all offsets within the method, thereby holding up=20
the rebalance until those commits are done. The (perceived) feature gap=20
is when the user is trying to do async commits from the rebalance=20
listener; there is nothing available to trigger the callbacks of=20
completed commits. Without these callback, there is no way to know when=20
it is safe to return from onPartitionsRevoked. (We cannot call `poll`=20
because the rebalance listener is already called from inside a poll.)

Calling `commitAsync` with an empty offsets parameter seems a perfect=20
candidate for triggering callbacks of earlier commits. Unfortunately,=20
commitAsync doesn't behave that way. This is fixed by mentioned pull=20
request.

The pull request conversation has a comment saying that calling `commit`=20
with an empty offsets parameter is not something that should happen. I=20
found this a strange thing to say. First of all, the method does have=20
special handling for this situation, negating the comment outright. In=20
addition this special handling violates the contract of the method (as=20
specified in the javadoc section about ordering). Therefore, this pull=20
request has 2 advantages:

 1. KafkaConsumer.commitAsync will be more in line with its javadoc,
 2. the feature gap is gone.

Of course, it might be that I missed something and that there are other=20
ways to trigger the commit callbacks. I would be very happy to hear=20
about that because it means I do not have to wait for a release cycle.

If you agree these arguments are sound, I would be happy to make the=20
pull request mergable again.

Curious to your thoughts and kind regards,
 =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 Erik.


--=20
Erik van Oosten
e.vanoosten@grons.nl
https://day-to-day-stuff.blogspot.com
