Hi Philip,

> Firstly, could you explain the situation
> in that you would prefer to invoke commitAsync over commitSync in the
> rebalance listener?

Of course!

Short answer: we prefer commitAsync because we want to handle multiple=20
partitions concurrently using the ZIO runtime.

Long answer: this is in the context of zio-kafka. In zio-kafka the user=20
writes code for a stream that processes data and does commits. In the=20
library we intercept those commits and pass them to the KafkaConsumer.=20
We also keep track of the offsets of handed out records. Together this=20
information allows us to track when a stream is ready processing a=20
partition and that it is safe to start the rebalance.

All of this happens concurrently and asynchronously using the ZIO=20
runtime. When calling commit inside the onPartitionRevoked callback the=20
library does a thread-id check; we can only call the KafkaConsumer from=20
the same thread that invoked us. This is unfortunate because it forces=20
us to spin up a specialized single-threaded ZIO runtime inside the=20
callback method. Though this runtime can run blocking methods like=20
commitSync, it will need careful programming since all other tasks need=20
to wait.

(BTW, it would be great if there is an option to disable the thread-id=20
check. It has more use cases, see for example KAFKA-7143.)

> is it your concern that we
> currently don't have a way to invoke the callback, and the user won't=20
> be to
> correctly handle these failed/successful async commits?

Yes, that is correct.

> Sorry - I dug a bit into the old PR. Seems like the issue is there's=20
> broken
> contract that if the commitSync won't wait for the previous async=20
> commits
> to complete if it tries to commit an empty offset map.

Indeed! I am assuming the same is true for commitAsync. The important=20
thing is that we need something to get those callbacks. I would prefer=20
commitAsync but if only commitSync gets fixed we can use that as well.=20
If there is another method completely for this task, that would be good=20
as well.

Kind regards,
     Erik.


Philip Nee schreef op 2023-05-02 21:49:
> Hey Erik,
>=20
> Just a couple of questions to you: Firstly, could you explain the=20
> situation
> in that you would prefer to invoke commitAsync over commitSync in the
> rebalance listener?  Typically we would use the synchronized method to
> ensure the commits are completed before moving on with the rebalancing,
> which leads to my second comment/question.  is it your concern that we
> currently don't have a way to invoke the callback, and the user won't=20
> be to
> correctly handle these failed/successful async commits?
>=20
> Thanks,
> P
>=20
> On Tue, May 2, 2023 at 12:22=E2=80=AFPM Erik van Oosten
> <e.vanoosten@grons.nl.invalid> wrote:
>=20
>> Dear developers of the Kafka java client,
>>=20
>> It seems I have found a feature gap in the Kafka java client.
>> KAFKA-10337 and its associated pull request on Github (from 2020!)=20
>> would
>> solve this, but it was closed without merging. We would love to see it
>> being reconsidered for merging. This mail has the arguments for doing=20
>> so.
>>=20
>> The javadoc of `ConsumerRebalanceListener` method=20
>> `onPartitionsRevoked`
>> recommends you commit all offsets within the method, thereby holding=20
>> up
>> the rebalance until those commits are done. The (perceived) feature=20
>> gap
>> is when the user is trying to do async commits from the rebalance
>> listener; there is nothing available to trigger the callbacks of
>> completed commits. Without these callback, there is no way to know=20
>> when
>> it is safe to return from onPartitionsRevoked. (We cannot call `poll`
>> because the rebalance listener is already called from inside a poll.)
>>=20
>> Calling `commitAsync` with an empty offsets parameter seems a perfect
>> candidate for triggering callbacks of earlier commits. Unfortunately,
>> commitAsync doesn't behave that way. This is fixed by mentioned pull
>> request.
>>=20
>> The pull request conversation has a comment saying that calling=20
>> `commit`
>> with an empty offsets parameter is not something that should happen. I
>> found this a strange thing to say. First of all, the method does have
>> special handling for this situation, negating the comment outright. In
>> addition this special handling violates the contract of the method (as
>> specified in the javadoc section about ordering). Therefore, this pull
>> request has 2 advantages:
>>=20
>>  1. KafkaConsumer.commitAsync will be more in line with its javadoc,
>>  2. the feature gap is gone.
>>=20
>> Of course, it might be that I missed something and that there are=20
>> other
>> ways to trigger the commit callbacks. I would be very happy to hear
>> about that because it means I do not have to wait for a release cycle.
>>=20
>> If you agree these arguments are sound, I would be happy to make the
>> pull request mergable again.
>>=20
>> Curious to your thoughts and kind regards,
>>      Erik.
>>=20
>>=20
